{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012aKLX7ENfDxTnMsz4kpe63","type":"tool_result","content":"# What you can't do with network policies (at least, not yet)\n\nNetworkPolicies do not yet support the following, although alternatives that provide similar functionality may be available:\n\n- forcing traffic through a single \"egress gateway\" (if you want to ensure that traffic from a Pod never leaves the cluster, or to add a single point of enforcement for egress traffic, you can use egress traffic shaping within the cluster or deploy a sidecar proxy in each applicable Pod)\n- TLS termination / SNI (if you want to control traffic based on the TLS SNI or the hostname of the target of a connection, you cannot do this with NetworkPolicies today; you will need to use some form of proxy for this purpose)\n- protocols other than TCP, UDP, and SCTP (if you want to control traffic for other protocols, you cannot do this with NetworkPolicies today)\n- an \"deny\" or \"blacklist\" policy (NetworkPolicies can only define allow rules; there is no concept of a \"deny\" rule)\n- instantaneously applying or updating a policy to a cluster (when you create or update a NetworkPolicy, it may take some time before it is applied to all Pods in the cluster)\n- matching high layer (OSI model layer 7) constructs or application-level constructs (you cannot write NetworkPolicy rules that say \"allow this HTTP request\" or \"allow this request if the user name is alice\"; you can only write rules that say \"allow connections on port 8080 from pods with this label\")"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"087b09f1-914c-4211-8f4f-c2844f53e37c","uuid":"41091694-39d7-422e-acb6-4818ffaccbf9","timestamp":"2026-08-25T03:49:03.237Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":544312,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# What you can't do with network policies (at least, not yet)\n\nNetworkPolicies do not yet support the following, although alternatives that provide similar functionality may be available:\n\n- forcing traffic through a single \"egress gateway\" (if you want to ensure that traffic from a Pod never leaves the cluster, or to add a single point of enforcement for egress traffic, you can use egress traffic shaping within the cluster or deploy a sidecar proxy in each applicable Pod)\n- TLS termination / SNI (if you want to control traffic based on the TLS SNI or the hostname of the target of a connection, you cannot do this with NetworkPolicies today; you will need to use some form of proxy for this purpose)\n- protocols other than TCP, UDP, and SCTP (if you want to control traffic for other protocols, you cannot do this with NetworkPolicies today)\n- an \"deny\" or \"blacklist\" policy (NetworkPolicies can only define allow rules; there is no concept of a \"deny\" rule)\n- instantaneously applying or updating a policy to a cluster (when you create or update a NetworkPolicy, it may take some time before it is applied to all Pods in the cluster)\n- matching high layer (OSI model layer 7) constructs or application-level constructs (you cannot write NetworkPolicy rules that say \"allow this HTTP request\" or \"allow this request if the user name is alice\"; you can only write rules that say \"allow connections on port 8080 from pods with this label\")","durationMs":5022,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies/"}}
