{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NbKYxoRJR8PZQQz39Lq954","type":"tool_result","content":"# NetworkPolicy Documentation Analysis\n\n## \"What you can't do with network policies\" Section\n\n**Exact heading:** \"What you can't do with network policies (at least, not yet)\"\n\n**Introductory sentence:** \"As of Kubernetes {{< skew currentVersion >}}, the following functionality does not exist in the NetworkPolicy API, but you might be able to implement workarounds using Operating System components (such as SELinux, OpenVSwitch, IPTables, and so on) or Layer 7 technologies (Ingress controllers, Service Mesh implementations) or admission controllers. In case you are new to network security in Kubernetes, its worth noting that the following User Stories cannot (yet) be implemented using the NetworkPolicy API.\"\n\n**Exact number of bullet items:** 11\n\n**Bullet points (in order):**\n\n1. \"Forcing internal cluster traffic to go through a common gateway (this might be best served with a service mesh or other proxy).\"\n2. \"Anything TLS related (use a service mesh or ingress controller for this).\"\n3. \"Node specific policies (you can use CIDR notation for these, but you cannot target nodes by their Kubernetes identities specifically).\"\n4. \"Targeting of services by name (you can, however, target pods or namespaces by their {{< glossary_tooltip text=\"labels\" term_id=\"label\" >}}, which is often a viable workaround).\"\n5. \"Creation or management of \"Policy requests\" that are fulfilled by a third party.\"\n6. \"Default policies which are applied to all namespaces or pods (there are some third party Kubernetes distributions and projects which can do this).\"\n7. \"Advanced policy querying and reachability tooling.\"\n8. \"The ability to log network security events (for example connections that are blocked or accepted).\"\n9. \"The ability to explicitly deny policies (currently the model for NetworkPolicies are deny by default, with only the ability to add allow rules).\"\n10. \"The ability to prevent loopback or incoming host traffic (Pods cannot currently block localhost access, nor do they have the ability to block access from their resident node).\"\n\n---\n\n## \"Targeting a Namespace by its name\" Section\n\n```markdown\n## Targeting a Namespace by its name\n\nThe Kubernetes control plane sets an immutable label `kubernetes.io/metadata.name` on all\nnamespaces, the value of the label is the namespace name.\n\nWhile NetworkPolicy cannot target a namespace by its name with some object field, you can use the\nstandardized label to target a specific namespace.\n```"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"087b09f1-914c-4211-8f4f-c2844f53e37c","uuid":"97a423e1-4a9b-4d93-b9ab-93d8dbfe9e5d","timestamp":"2026-08-25T03:49:47.863Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":23084,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# NetworkPolicy Documentation Analysis\n\n## \"What you can't do with network policies\" Section\n\n**Exact heading:** \"What you can't do with network policies (at least, not yet)\"\n\n**Introductory sentence:** \"As of Kubernetes {{< skew currentVersion >}}, the following functionality does not exist in the NetworkPolicy API, but you might be able to implement workarounds using Operating System components (such as SELinux, OpenVSwitch, IPTables, and so on) or Layer 7 technologies (Ingress controllers, Service Mesh implementations) or admission controllers. In case you are new to network security in Kubernetes, its worth noting that the following User Stories cannot (yet) be implemented using the NetworkPolicy API.\"\n\n**Exact number of bullet items:** 11\n\n**Bullet points (in order):**\n\n1. \"Forcing internal cluster traffic to go through a common gateway (this might be best served with a service mesh or other proxy).\"\n2. \"Anything TLS related (use a service mesh or ingress controller for this).\"\n3. \"Node specific policies (you can use CIDR notation for these, but you cannot target nodes by their Kubernetes identities specifically).\"\n4. \"Targeting of services by name (you can, however, target pods or namespaces by their {{< glossary_tooltip text=\"labels\" term_id=\"label\" >}}, which is often a viable workaround).\"\n5. \"Creation or management of \"Policy requests\" that are fulfilled by a third party.\"\n6. \"Default policies which are applied to all namespaces or pods (there are some third party Kubernetes distributions and projects which can do this).\"\n7. \"Advanced policy querying and reachability tooling.\"\n8. \"The ability to log network security events (for example connections that are blocked or accepted).\"\n9. \"The ability to explicitly deny policies (currently the model for NetworkPolicies are deny by default, with only the ability to add allow rules).\"\n10. \"The ability to prevent loopback or incoming host traffic (Pods cannot currently block localhost access, nor do they have the ability to block access from their resident node).\"\n\n---\n\n## \"Targeting a Namespace by its name\" Section\n\n```markdown\n## Targeting a Namespace by its name\n\nThe Kubernetes control plane sets an immutable label `kubernetes.io/metadata.name` on all\nnamespaces, the value of the label is the namespace name.\n\nWhile NetworkPolicy cannot target a namespace by its name with some object field, you can use the\nstandardized label to target a specific namespace.\n```","durationMs":4133,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies.md"}}
