{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0152kHGH5x77iaACrfmztowt","type":"tool_result","content":"## What you can't do with network policies (at least, not yet)\n\nAs of Kubernetes {{< skew currentVersion >}}, the following functionality does not exist in the\nNetworkPolicy API, but you might be able to implement workarounds using Operating System\ncomponents (such as SELinux, OpenVSwitch, IPTables, and so on) or Layer 7 technologies (Ingress\ncontrollers, Service Mesh implementations) or admission controllers. In case you are new to\nnetwork security in Kubernetes, its worth noting that the following User Stories cannot (yet) be\nimplemented using the NetworkPolicy API.\n\n- Forcing internal cluster traffic to go through a common gateway (this might be best served with\n  a service mesh or other proxy).\n- Anything TLS related (use a service mesh or ingress controller for this).\n- Node specific policies (you can use CIDR notation for these, but you cannot target nodes by\n  their Kubernetes identities specifically).\n- Targeting of services by name (you can, however, target pods or namespaces by their\n  {{< glossary_tooltip text=\"labels\" term_id=\"label\" >}}, which is often a viable workaround).\n- Creation or management of \"Policy requests\" that are fulfilled by a third party.\n- Default policies which are applied to all namespaces or pods (there are some third party\n  Kubernetes distributions and projects which can do this).\n- Advanced policy querying and reachability tooling.\n- The ability to log network security events (for example connections that are blocked or accepted).\n- The ability to explicitly deny policies (currently the model for NetworkPolicies are deny by\n  default, with only the ability to add allow rules).\n- The ability to prevent loopback or incoming host traffic (Pods cannot currently block localhost\n  access, nor do they have the ability to block access from their resident node).\n\n**Total bullet lines: 10**\n\n**Final bullet:** \"The ability to prevent loopback or incoming host traffic (Pods cannot currently block localhost access, nor do they have the ability to block access from their resident node).\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"087b09f1-914c-4211-8f4f-c2844f53e37c","uuid":"95815bf8-f8b4-441e-9d58-8d8b5fa2a512","timestamp":"2026-08-25T03:50:36.577Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":23084,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## What you can't do with network policies (at least, not yet)\n\nAs of Kubernetes {{< skew currentVersion >}}, the following functionality does not exist in the\nNetworkPolicy API, but you might be able to implement workarounds using Operating System\ncomponents (such as SELinux, OpenVSwitch, IPTables, and so on) or Layer 7 technologies (Ingress\ncontrollers, Service Mesh implementations) or admission controllers. In case you are new to\nnetwork security in Kubernetes, its worth noting that the following User Stories cannot (yet) be\nimplemented using the NetworkPolicy API.\n\n- Forcing internal cluster traffic to go through a common gateway (this might be best served with\n  a service mesh or other proxy).\n- Anything TLS related (use a service mesh or ingress controller for this).\n- Node specific policies (you can use CIDR notation for these, but you cannot target nodes by\n  their Kubernetes identities specifically).\n- Targeting of services by name (you can, however, target pods or namespaces by their\n  {{< glossary_tooltip text=\"labels\" term_id=\"label\" >}}, which is often a viable workaround).\n- Creation or management of \"Policy requests\" that are fulfilled by a third party.\n- Default policies which are applied to all namespaces or pods (there are some third party\n  Kubernetes distributions and projects which can do this).\n- Advanced policy querying and reachability tooling.\n- The ability to log network security events (for example connections that are blocked or accepted).\n- The ability to explicitly deny policies (currently the model for NetworkPolicies are deny by\n  default, with only the ability to add allow rules).\n- The ability to prevent loopback or incoming host traffic (Pods cannot currently block localhost\n  access, nor do they have the ability to block access from their resident node).\n\n**Total bullet lines: 10**\n\n**Final bullet:** \"The ability to prevent loopback or incoming host traffic (Pods cannot currently block localhost access, nor do they have the ability to block access from their resident node).\"","durationMs":2753,"url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/services-networking/network-policies.md"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E3zNjCs9fM3krJq5YbLcNa","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"north-south traffic\" \"east-west traffic\" definition Kubernetes cluster networking\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Seven zero trust rules for Kubernetes | CNCF\",\"url\":\"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2022/11/04/seven-zero-trust-rules-for-kubernetes/\"},{\"title\":\"See Networking and Traffic Flow in Cloud Native environments at CNCF Cloud Native Singapore\",\"url\":\"https://community.cncf.io/events/details/cncf-singapore-kubernetes-user-group-presents-networking-and-traffic-flow-in-cloud-native-environments/\"},{\"title\":\"Gateway API 1.4: New Features | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/06/gateway-api-v1-4/\"},{\"title\":\"Services, Load Balancing, and Networking | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/\"},{\"title\":\"Cluster Networking | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/networking/\"},{\"title\":\"Istio / Getting started with the Kubernetes Gateway API\",\"url\":\"https://istio.io/latest/blog/2022/getting-started-gtwapi/\"},{\"title\":\"Federating clusters for zero-downtime Kubernetes | CNCF\",\"url\":\"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/07/27/federating-clusters-for-zero-downtime-kubernetes/\"},{\"title\":\"Gateway API v0.8.0: Introducing Service Mesh Support | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2023/08/29/gateway-api-v0-8/\"},{\"title\":\"Topology Aware Routing | Kubernetes\",\"url\":\"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/topology-aware-routing/\"},{\"title\":\"Istio / Traffic Distribution\",\"url\":\"https://istio.io/latest/docs/ambient/usage/traffic-distribution/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here are the definitions of north-south and east-west traffic in Kubernetes cluster networking:\n\n## East-West Traffic\n\nIn Kubernetes, east-west traffic is the majority – most applications communicate via APIs and adopt microservice or small application paradigms. More specifically, east-west traffic refers to service-to-service traffic management within a cluster.\n\n## North-South Traffic\n\nNorth-south traffic refers to traffic entering or leaving the cluster. Ingress controllers manage north-south traffic and are the logical point for continuous authentication of external APIs interacting with internal services.\n\n## Context in Kubernetes Networking\n\nThese terms describe different directions of network traffic flow within Kubernetes infrastructure. While the initial focus of Gateway API was always ingress (north-south) traffic, it was clear almost from the beginning that the same basic routing concepts should also be applicable to service mesh (east-west) traffic.\n\nIn summary, north-south traffic is external communication flowing in and out of the cluster, while east-west traffic is internal communication between services within the cluster.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"087b09f1-914c-4211-8f4f-c2844f53e37c","uuid":"5d950974-9f69-4a79-9ad6-057eadfd25b0","timestamp":"2026-08-25T03:50:41.078Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"north-south traffic\" \"east-west traffic\" definition Kubernetes cluster networking","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Q3eJGH2qtMtF9B16ZgKUyn","content":[{"title":"Seven zero trust rules for Kubernetes | CNCF","url":"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2022/11/04/seven-zero-trust-rules-for-kubernetes/"},{"title":"See Networking and Traffic Flow in Cloud Native environments at CNCF Cloud Native Singapore","url":"https://community.cncf.io/events/details/cncf-singapore-kubernetes-user-group-presents-networking-and-traffic-flow-in-cloud-native-environments/"},{"title":"Gateway API 1.4: New Features | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2025/11/06/gateway-api-v1-4/"},{"title":"Services, Load Balancing, and Networking | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/"},{"title":"Cluster Networking | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/cluster-administration/networking/"},{"title":"Istio / Getting started with the Kubernetes Gateway API","url":"https://istio.io/latest/blog/2022/getting-started-gtwapi/"},{"title":"Federating clusters for zero-downtime Kubernetes | CNCF","url":"https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/07/27/federating-clusters-for-zero-downtime-kubernetes/"},{"title":"Gateway API v0.8.0: Introducing Service Mesh Support | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/blog/2023/08/29/gateway-api-v0-8/"},{"title":"Topology Aware Routing | Kubernetes","url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/topology-aware-routing/"},{"title":"Istio / Traffic Distribution","url":"https://istio.io/latest/docs/ambient/usage/traffic-distribution/"}]},"Based on the search results, here are the definitions of north-south and east-west traffic in Kubernetes cluster networking:\n\n## East-West Traffic\n\nIn Kubernetes, east-west traffic is the majority – most applications communicate via APIs and adopt microservice or small application paradigms. More specifically, east-west traffic refers to service-to-service traffic management within a cluster.\n\n## North-South Traffic\n\nNorth-south traffic refers to traffic entering or leaving the cluster. Ingress controllers manage north-south traffic and are the logical point for continuous authentication of external APIs interacting with internal services.\n\n## Context in Kubernetes Networking\n\nThese terms describe different directions of network traffic flow within Kubernetes infrastructure. While the initial focus of Gateway API was always ingress (north-south) traffic, it was clear almost from the beginning that the same basic routing concepts should also be applicable to service mesh (east-west) traffic.\n\nIn summary, north-south traffic is external communication flowing in and out of the cluster, while east-west traffic is internal communication between services within the cluster."],"durationSeconds":5.258615000000049,"searchCount":1}}
